;; ============================================================================

;; FUNCTIONS AND CALLS  —  port of Simple's FunNode, ParmNode, CallNode,
;;                         CallEndNode, FunPtrNode, EscapeNode
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; RESERVED-NAME HAZARD: `call` and `block` are Coil builtins. Nothing here may be named `call`;
;; every entry point is `call-*` or `*-call`.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE ONE IDEA: A Fun IS a Region. A Parm IS a Phi.
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Simple's `FunNode extends RegionNode`, and `ParmNode extends PhiNode`. That is not a code-reuse
;; convenience — it is the design, and almost everything below is a consequence:
;;
;;   - A function's inputs are the CALL SITES that reach it. It is a merge, exactly like any other.
;;   - A parameter is a Phi over those inputs. Its type is the meet of what every reaching caller
;;     passes, computed by the SAME `compute` every Phi uses.
;;   - Therefore INTERPROCEDURAL TYPE INFERENCE IS NOT A PASS. It falls out of the ordinary
;;     dataflow solver, because a parameter is already a merge and the solver already handles
;;     merges. This is what makes the closed-world argument affordable.
;;   - Therefore INLINING IS NOT A PASS EITHER. See below.
;;
;; If we invent a different call representation, all three of those properties have to be rebuilt
;; by hand as bespoke passes. Do not.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; INLINING  —  Simple's `CallEndNode.maybeInline` / `doInline`, and `IterPeeps.iterate`
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; ## It is not a pass; it is a second worklist in the peephole fixpoint
;;
;; `IterPeeps` carries a worklist of CallEnd nodes alongside the ordinary node worklist, and ONE
;; loop interleaves them:
;;
;;     loop:
;;       run peepholes to a fixpoint          (everything that does not grow the code)
;;       pop inline candidates until one FIRES (first come, first served — no ranking)
;;       if one fired: goto loop              (re-clean, then look again)
;;       if none fired and nothing ever fired this round: done
;;       otherwise: re-queue the DEFERRED candidates and go round again
;;
;; Note there is no cost model choosing an order. Simple's own comment: "no real heuristic, first
;; come, first served". The precision comes from the call graph SCCP discovered, not from ranking.
;;
;; ## Trivial inlining CLONES NOTHING — read this twice
;;
;; Simple's comment: "Inlining does not need to clone code, merely triggers folding the Call/Fun
;; and Return/CallEnd away." The entire operation is:
;;
;;     cend._folding = true
;;     fun._folding  = true
;;     fun.setDef(1, call.ctrl())   ; the Fun's control now bypasses the Call
;;     fun.ret().setDef(3, null)    ; the Return's RPC edge is consumed
;;     re-queue fun, cend, and their neighbours (and the Parms' users, one layer down)
;;     invalidate the idepth caches en masse
;;
;; ...and then it STOPS. The ordinary peepholes do the rest, because a Fun is a Region and a Parm
;; is a Phi:
;;
;;     the Fun now has ONE input        -> Region rule 3 collapses it into the caller's control
;;     each Parm now has ONE value      -> Phi rule 1 collapses it into the caller's argument
;;
;; Argument substitution, control splicing and return merging are all just those two existing
;; rules firing. There is no inliner body to write.
;;
;; ## Cloning is a PRE-STEP, not a second mechanism
;;
;; When the callee has other callers, a trivial fold would steal the body from them. So `_inline==2`
;; first: unlink the call, `fun.copyBody()`, build a fresh FunPtr over the clone with an
;; INLINE-ONLY function index (one that is never written to an object file), relink the call to the
;; clone — and then run the SAME trivial inline on it. One mechanism, always.
;;
;; ## The decision is a tri-state that DEFERS rather than declines
;;
;;    1  inline now, trivially
;;    2  clone the body first, then inline trivially
;;   -1  NOT NOW, but might become a candidate — re-queued via `addDep` on whatever blocked it
;;   -2  never; stop asking
;;
;; That `-1` is our own "deferred, never declined" law appearing in Simple's code. What produces it:
;;
;;   - `nIns()!=2` — not exactly one linked target yet. SCCP may still link one.
;;   - the function pointer is not yet a CONSTANT, non-null TypeFunPtr. `addDep(fptr)`.
;;   - the arguments do not yet `isa` the formals. `addDep(arg)`.
;;   - the body is currently too big, but cleanup might shrink it below the cap.
;;   - the callee is self-recursive right now — but might lose the recursive edge. `addDep(fun)`.
;;
;; And what produces `-2` (never) in the implemented IR: the CallEnd is already folding; input 0 is
;; not a Call (malformed, mid-death); the `_noInline` suffix; or the construction span exceeds the
;; hard cap. A large cleaned live body is `-1`, above, because cleanup can shrink it.
;;
;; ## The implemented heuristic
;;
;;   - size cap 100 nodes, checked against both the dense Fun-through-Return construction span and
;;     the current CFG-bounded live-body cardinality;
;;   - NEVER inline a self-recursive function — that is loop unrolling by another name;
;;   - require exactly ONE linked target with a constant, non-null function pointer;
;;   - require every argument's type to `isa` its formal, against the recursively upgraded formal
;;     so that deep mutability requirements participate before inlining.
;;
;; Simple additionally grants selected initializers a 200-node cap. That requires frontend
;; initializer/class-body classification evidence that this repository does not yet have; it is
;; recorded as a blocked difference rather than silently claimed here.
;;
;; ## Consequence for JSL — there is no JSL inlining question
;;
;; A JSL definition lowers to an ordinary Fun graph. It inlines through this same CallEnd worklist,
;; this same tri-state, and this same fold. `JsAdd` on two proven integers therefore specialises to
;; a single Add through mechanisms that already exist for user functions. That is the property that
;; makes the JSL design work, and it is why there is no JSL-specific machinery downstream of
;; src/jsl/lower.coil. See docs/FRONTEND.md.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE CALL GRAPH IS DISCOVERED, NOT DECLARED
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; During optimistic SCCP (src/codegen/opto.coil), as a Call becomes REACHABLE — its control is
;; neither TOP nor XCONTROL — it LINKS against the function pointers reaching it: an edge Call->Fun
;; and an edge Return->CallEnd. Because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is a Phi, argument and return
;; types then propagate along ordinary edges with no special rule.
;;
;; The result is a precise call graph in linear time, computed by the same solver as everything
;; else. Under the closed-world assumption it is COMPLETE, and that completeness is what upgrades a
;; TypeScript annotation from a hint to a fact we can discharge.
;;
;; Before machine lowering the calls are UNLINKED again (the Unlink phase), because the machine
;; representation wants explicit call sequences rather than a merge.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; THE NODES
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; FUN      a Region whose inputs are call sites. Carries a signature and a function index (fidx).
;; PARM     a Phi on a Fun, with a declared parameter type as its constraint.
;; CALL     a block TAIL and a multi: control, memory, arguments, and the function pointer LAST.
;; CALLEND  a block HEAD and a multi: the result and the new memory project out of it.
;; FUNPTR   a constant naming a Fun. `TypeFunPtr` carries a SET of function indices, so a call site
;;          whose set is a singleton is a direct call.
;; ESCAPE   a reference leaving the analysed region; nothing may be assumed about it afterwards.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; STATUS: implemented. Fun-as-Region, Parm-as-Phi, Call, CallEnd, FunPtr, linking, the tri-state
;; decision, trivial inlining by folding, the clone pre-step for shared bodies, and full memory
;; threading through Call/Return/CallEnd state tuples.
;; ============================================================================
(module aot.node.call)
(import "coil.primitive" :as primitive)
(import "coil.alloc" :as alloc :use [unwrap-ptr create box!])
(import "coil.arraylist" :use *)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)
(import "aot.node.property" :use *)
(import "aot.node.cfg" :use *)
(import "aot.node.control" :use *)
(import "aot.node.phi" :use *)
(import "aot.type.type" :use *)

;; The inline decision, matching Simple's byte return exactly.
(const INLINE-NEVER -2)

(const INLINE-DEFER -1)

(const INLINE-TRIVIAL 1)

(const INLINE-CLONE 2)

;; Simple's size caps.
(const INLINE-MAX-SIZE 100)

(const INLINE-MAX-SIZE-INIT 200)

;; A JSL body this small (a boxing shim, a one-test guard) inlines without evidence: the call
;; would cost more than the body.
(const INLINE-TINY-SIZE 12)

;; Body ownership lives in `aot.node.copy`, which imports this module. Register its cleaned-body
;; cardinality query at boot instead of creating a cyclic import. Candidate selection must not fall
;; back to the construction span: optimizer-created growth can make that historical number stale.
(defstruct FunBodySizeExt
  [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps))] i64)) (installed bool)])

(defn fun-body-size-ext [] (-> (ptr FunBodySizeExt))
  (primitive/alloc-static FunBodySizeExt))

(defn set-fun-body-size-ext! [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps))] i64))] (-> i64)
  (do
    (set! (.f (fun-body-size-ext)) f)
    (set! (.installed (fun-body-size-ext)) true)
    0))

;; The inlining epoch: bumped after every inline that fires. A body's live size is walked at most
;; once per epoch per Fun — every deferred candidate asks again each round, and the walk is the
;; body's size; asking per candidate made the inline decision a third of a whole compile. A size
;; measured earlier in the epoch can only be stale upward (peepholes shrink bodies), which delays
;; an inline to the next epoch and never admits one wrongly.
(defn fun-size-epoch [] (-> (ptr i64))
  (primitive/alloc-static i64))

(defn fun-size-epoch-bump! [] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (fun-size-epoch) (+ (load (fun-size-epoch)) 1)) 0))

(defn fun-live-body-size [(fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (.installed (fun-body-size-ext))
      (let [p (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr fun))
            epoch (+ (load (fun-size-epoch)) 1)]
        (if (= (.live-size-epoch p) epoch)
            (.live-size p)
            (let [size (primitive/call-ptr (.f (fun-body-size-ext)) fun)]
              (set! (.live-size p) size)
              (set! (.live-size-epoch p) epoch)
              size)))
      (do
        (panic "fun-live-body-size: body-selection extension was not installed at boot")
        0)))

(defstruct CloneInlineExt
  [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps)) (ref (dyn NodeOps))] i64)) (installed bool)])

(defn clone-inline-ext [] (-> (ptr CloneInlineExt))
  (primitive/alloc-static CloneInlineExt))

(defn set-clone-inline-ext! [(f (fnptr c
                                       [(ref (dyn NodeOps)) (ref (dyn NodeOps))]
                                       i64))] (-> i64)
  (do
    (set! (.f (clone-inline-ext)) f)
    (set! (.installed (clone-inline-ext)) true)
    0))

(defn callend-do-clone-inline-ext! [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (.installed (clone-inline-ext))
      (primitive/call-ptr (.f (clone-inline-ext)) e fun)
      (do
        (panic "callend-do-clone-inline-ext!: graph-copy extension was not installed at boot")
        0)))

;; Call input layout, matching Simple exactly:
;;   0 = control, 1 = memory, 2.. = arguments, LAST = the function pointer.
;;
;; The memory slot always carries the caller's current memory state in production lowering. Keeping
;; it at Simple's exact index makes argument indices shared with `Parm`; renumbering either side
;; would silently rewire every parameter.
(const CALL-CTRL 0)

(const CALL-MEM 1)

(const CALL-ARG0 2)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; FUN — a Region whose inputs are the CALL SITES that reach it
;;
;; Input 1 is either `Start` — the UNKNOWN-CALLER hook, meaning "anyone at all might call this" — or
;; a real call site. Inputs 2.. are the linked callers.
(defstruct FunNode
  [(hdr NodeHdr)
   (sig i64)  ; the signature; sharpens over time as the Return's type improves
   (name (slice u8))
   (folding bool)  ; set while this Fun is being trivially inlined away
   (escapes bool)  ; its pointer was materialized as a value: the address must stay defined
   (approx-size i64)  ; approximate body size, the inlining heuristic
   (live-size i64)  ; the last measured live body size (see `fun-live-body-size`)
   (live-size-epoch i64)  ; the inlining epoch that measurement belongs to; 0 = never
   (self-recursive bool)  ; the last answer of `fun-self-recursive?`
   (self-recursive-epoch i64)  ; and the epoch it belongs to; 0 = never
   (jsl bool)  ; a JSL definition: a generic abstract operation, inlined only on evidence
   (ret-nid i64)])  ; the Return, by nid — a back-pointer that survives edge surgery

(defn fun-sig [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.sig (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-name [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (slice u8))
  (.name (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-no-inline? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [name (fun-name f)
        suffix "_noInline"
        n (len name)
        sn (len suffix)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut same) (>= n sn)]
    (loop (if (or (not (load same)) (>= (load i) sn))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (if (!= (get name (+ (- n sn) (load i))) (get suffix (load i)))
                    (set! (mut same) false)
                    0)
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load same)))

;; A function whose pointer escaped into a value. Simple inlines a function only when "its
;; function pointer is only used here"; an escaped pointer is used elsewhere by definition, and the
;; body must remain emitted so the relocated address resolves.
;; JSL ownership (docs/DECISIONS.md, the compile-time architecture, decision 3).
(defn fun-jsl? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.jsl (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-set-jsl! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.jsl (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) true) 0))

(defn fun-escapes? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.escapes (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-set-escapes! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.escapes (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) true) 0))

(defn fun-folding? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

(defn fun-approx-size [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.approx-size (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))))

;; Hoisted frontends declare every Fun header before lowering any body. Their nid span therefore
;; includes unrelated headers and is not the construction-size estimate final Simple gets from
;; parsing one function at a time. Those frontends overwrite the provisional span with the exact
;; number of nodes allocated while lowering this body.
(defn fun-set-approx-size! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (size i64)] (-> i64)
  (if (< size 0)
      (panic-i "fun-set-approx-size!: negative body size" size)
      (do
        (set! (.approx-size (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) size)
        0)))

(defn fun-set-folding! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (v bool)] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) v) 0))

;; The signature only ever SHARPENS. `n-unlock!` first: the signature participates in nothing today,
;; but the Fun is a Region whose GVN identity depends on its edges, and Simple unlocks here too.
(defn fun-set-sig! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64)] (-> i64)
  (if (= (fun-sig f) sig)
      0
      (do
        (n-unlock! f)
        (set! (.sig (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))) sig)
        (funptr-refresh-from-fun! f sig)
        (code-add! f)
        (code-add-outputs! f)
        0)))

(defn fun-ret [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (arena-node (.ret-nid (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f)))))

(defn fun-linkable? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  ;; Final Simple's `CodeGen.link(fidx)` is statically typed to return a FunNode. Here a FunPtr keeps
  ;; the target as an ordinary graph edge, and inlining can rewrite that edge one worklist turn
  ;; before every former Call has disappeared. Establish the same type guarantee before touching
  ;; Fun-only payload; a stale non-Fun target is simply no longer linkable.
  (and (= (n-op f) OP-FUN)
       (and (not (n-dead? f))
            (and (not (fun-folding? f))
                 (match (fun-ret f)
                   (Some [ret]
                         (and (!= (n-ty ret) TY-INVALID) (not (n-dead? ret))))
                   (None [] false))))))

;; Replace optimization-only caller edges with a runtime entry and architectural RPC parameter.
;; Source and JSL functions share this final-Simple handoff so their backend invariants cannot
;; drift apart.
(defn fun-prepare-codegen! [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (start (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (or (n-dead? fun) (fun-folding? fun) (ty-high? (n-ty fun)))
      0
      (do
        (if (not (fun-unknown-callers? fun))
            (do
              (n-insert-def! fun 1 (Some start))
              (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
                    (mut i) 0]
                (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                          (break 0)
                          (do
                            (match (n-out fun (load i))
                              (Some [u]
                                    (if (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                                        (n-insert-def! u
                                                       1
                                                       (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
                                        0))
                              (None [] 0))
                            (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                            (continue))))))
            0)
        (match (fun-rpc-parm fun)
          (Some [rpc]
                (match (fun-ret fun)
                  (Some [ret] (n-set-def! ret RETURN-RPC (Some rpc)))
                  (None []
                        (panic "fun-prepare-codegen!: live Fun has no Return"))))
          (None []
                (let [rpc (parm-make-slot fun 0 (ty-int-bot) "$rpc")]
                  (match (fun-ret fun)
                    (Some [ret] (n-set-def! ret RETURN-RPC (Some rpc)))
                    (None []
                          (panic "fun-prepare-codegen!: live Fun has no Return"))))))
        1)))

;; Cannot build the Return and the Fun at once; one has to come first. So the link is a second step,
;; exactly as in Simple.
(defn fun-set-ret! [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [p (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))]
    (set! (.ret-nid p) (n-nid ret))
    ;; Simple records the parser UID delta from Fun creation through Return creation. Node IDs are
    ;; that same dense construction clock here, so this includes every node built while the body was
    ;; open (including nested functions) without needing ownership metadata on every floating node.
    (set! (.approx-size p) (+ 1 (- (n-nid ret) (n-nid f))))
    0))

;; >>> THE UNKNOWN-CALLER HOOK <<<
;;
;; While input 1 is `Start`, this function might be called by ANYONE. Its parameters can be anything,
;; so it reports CONTROL unconditionally and its Parms report their declared types.
;;
;; Removing that hook is what the closed world buys: once SCCP has proven the complete set of callers
;; and the function does not escape, the hook goes and the parameter types become the meet of what
;; the actual callers pass. That is the moment a TypeScript annotation becomes provable.
(defn fun-unknown-callers? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (and (>= (n-nins f) 2)
       (match (n-in f 1) (Some [v] (= (n-op v) OP-START)) (None [] false))))

;; Simple's `FunNode.compute`: CONTROL while callers are unknown, else the meet over real callers.
;; The Start hook is SKIPPED in the meet — it is a hook, not a caller.
(defn fun-compute [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (fun-unknown-callers? f)
      TY-CTRL
      (let [cnt (n-nins f)
            (mut i) 1
            (mut t) TY-XCTRL]
        (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                  (break 0)
                  (do
                    (match (n-in f (load i))
                      (Some [v]
                            (if (= (n-op v) OP-START)
                                0
                                (set! (mut t) (ty-meet (load t) (n-ty v)))))
                      (None [] 0))
                    (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                    (continue))))
        (load t))))

;; A concrete call-graph link has two inseparable halves: Call->Fun (plus aligned Parm arms) and
;; Return->CallEnd. Generic Region dead-path deletion only knows about the first half, leaving a
;; stale Return on CallEnd that can later trigger inlining after the caller edge is gone. Remove a
;; dead concrete caller through `call-unlink!`, which updates both halves atomically. The Start hook
;; remains governed solely by closed-world transition code.
(defn fun--dead-caller! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [path (region--find-dead-input f (if (fun-unknown-callers? f) 2 1))]
    (if (= path 0)
        (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
        (let [caller (n-in! f path)]
          (if (= (n-op caller) OP-CALL)
              (call-unlink! caller f)
              (region-del-path! f path))
          (if (= (n-nins f) 1) (Some (xctrl-make)) (Some f))))))

(defn fun-idealize [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  ;; 1. A linked caller died. Skip input 1 while it is the unknown-caller hook: dropping the hook is
  ;; a separate, deliberate decision (see below), not something dead-path removal may do. Concrete
  ;; callers unlink atomically with their CallEnd Return edge.
  (match (fun--dead-caller! f)
    (Some [x] (Some x))
    (None []
          ;; 2. Upgrade the signature from the actual return type, when it sharpened.
          (match (fun-ret f)
            (Some [ret]
                  (let [rt (n-ty ret)]
                    ;; Return can finish folding one worklist turn before its Fun. Its arena slot
                    ;; remains addressable for ownership metadata, but TY-INVALID is not a lattice
                    ;; value and must not be inspected as one; proceed directly to Fun collapse.
                    (if (= rt TY-INVALID)
                        (fun--maybe-collapse f)
                        (if (and (ty-tuple? rt)
                                 (> (ty-tuple-len rt) RETURN-VALUE))
                            (let [actual (ty-tuple-at rt RETURN-VALUE)
                                  declared (ty-sig-ret (fun-sig f))]
                              (if (= actual TY-INVALID)
                                  (panic-i "fun-idealize: return tuple contains TY_INVALID; fun nid"
                                           (n-nid f))
                                  0)
                              (if (and (!= actual declared)
                                       (ty-isa? actual declared))
                                  (do
                                    (fun-set-sig! f
                                                  (fun--sig-with-ret (fun-sig f)
                                                                     actual))
                                    (Some f))
                                  (fun--maybe-collapse f)))
                            (fun--maybe-collapse f)))))
            (None [] (fun--maybe-collapse f))))))

;; >>> THE COLLAPSE THAT IS INLINING <<<
;;
;; A Fun that is FOLDING and down to one input, with no Phis (its Parms having collapsed already),
;; becomes that input. This is Region rule 3, and it is the second half of what makes trivial
;; inlining work: the Fun's control becomes the caller's control.
;;
;; Guarded on `folding`, because a Fun with one caller that is NOT being inlined must keep its shape —
;; it is still a function, and something may call it again.
(defn fun--detach-folding-fptrs! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  ;; FunPtr's target input is an implementation-only identity link. Final Simple carries only the
  ;; fidx constant and resolves it through CodeGen; therefore a collapsing Fun cannot rewrite a
  ;; pointer's target to the caller control. Leaving that edge attached lets a not-yet-collected
  ;; folding Call observe a caller Fun under the old fidx and install a bogus call-graph edge.
  (let [(mut i) 0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nouts f))
              (break 0)
              (match (n-out f (load i))
                (Some [use]
                      (if (and (= (n-op use) OP-FUNPTR)
                               (match (n-in use 0)
                                 (Some [target] (n-same? target f))
                                 (None [] false)))
                          (do
                            (n-del-def! use 0)
                            (code-add! use)
                            (code-add-outputs! use)
                            ;; The Fun's output list compressed; inspect this slot again.
                            (continue))
                          (do (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1)) (continue))))
                (None [] (do (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1)) (continue))))))))

(defn fun--maybe-collapse [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (fun-unknown-callers? f)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      (if (and (and (fun-folding? f) (= (n-nins f) 2))
               (not (region-has-phi? f)))
          (do
            (fun--detach-folding-fptrs! f)
            (n-in f 1))
          (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))

;; Replace the return member of a signature tuple.
(defn fun--sig-with-ret [(sig i64) (ret i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [n (ty-tuple-len sig)
        a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        (mut members) (al-new [i64] a)
        (mut i) 0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) n)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (push! [i64]
                       (mut members)
                       (if (= (load i) 0) ret (ty-tuple-at sig (load i))))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (let [answer (ty-tuple (al-slice (load members)))]
      (al-free! [i64] (mut members))
      answer)))

(impl NodeOps FunNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr FunNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr FunNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr FunNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (fun-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr FunNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (fun-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  ;; Function control identities never GVN. Final Simple carries the closed-world fidx in the
  ;; function signature, which distinguishes equal-shaped functions and inline clones. Our fidx
  ;; currently lives on FunPtr, so nid is the equivalent identity until compunit moves it onto Fun.
  ;; Comparing only the value signature incorrectly merges JsAdd/JsMul/JsSub after their Start
  ;; hooks close, because their control edges and tuple signatures are otherwise identical.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr FunNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (do self other false))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> i64) (.nid (.hdr self))))

(impl CFGOps FunNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr FunNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr FunNode))] (-> bool) true)
  ;; Final Simple deliberately bypasses Region dominance for a Fun.  Call edges can be recursive,
  ;; so taking their LCA would walk a cyclic interprocedural CFG.  A function is its own CFG root
  ;; except during an inline fold, when input 1 is the one caller being spliced into its body.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr FunNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (if (.folding self)
                (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 1)
                (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr FunNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (if (.folding self)
                  (region-idepth (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
                  1)))

;; Build a Fun with the unknown-caller hook already installed.
(defn fun-make [(start (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [FunNode] (alloc/create [FunNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.sig p) sig)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (set! (.folding p) false)
    (set! (.escapes p) false)
    (set! (.jsl p) false)
    (set! (.approx-size p) 0)
    (set! (.ret-nid p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-FUN)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; slot 0 unused, as in Region
    (n-add-def! d (Some start))  ; slot 1: the unknown-caller hook
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

;; Drop the unknown-caller hook. THE closed-world move: from here the parameter types are the meet of
;; what the actual callers pass, rather than "anything at all".
(defn fun-drop-unknown-callers! [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (not (fun-unknown-callers? f))
      (broken "fun-drop-unknown-callers!: there is no unknown-caller hook to drop")
      (do (region-del-path! f 1) 0)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; PARM — a Phi on a Fun, with a declared parameter type
;;
;; Input 0 is the Fun. Inputs 1.. are the matching argument from each caller, positionally aligned
;; with the Fun's inputs — which is why `region-del-path!` must count a Parm as a Phi.
;;
;; `_idx` maps one-to-one onto Call input indices. Slot 0 is RPC, slot 1 is memory, and source
;; arguments begin at slot 2. Public source-argument lookup subtracts CALL-ARG0; the stored identity
;; never uses a second numbering scheme.
;;
;; ## Why interprocedural inference is not a pass
;;
;; A Parm is a Phi, so its type is the MEET of what every reaching caller passes — computed by the
;; SAME `compute` every Phi uses. There is no separate propagation step. That is the entire payoff of
;; the Fun-is-a-Region design, and it is what makes the closed-world argument affordable.
;;
;; `constrain` joins with the DECLARED type: a parameter is never wider than its annotation says,
;; and never narrower than the callers justify. Where those two agree the annotation is discharged.
(defstruct ParmNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (declared i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defn parm-idx [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (- (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr p))) CALL-ARG0))

(defn parm-call-slot [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr p))))

(defn parm-declared [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.declared (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr p))))

(defn parm-fun [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! p 0))

;; A Parm is IN PROGRESS exactly while its Fun has unknown callers. Simple: "Always in-progress until
;; we run out of unknown callers."
(defn parm-in-progress? [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (match (n-in p 0)
    (Some [f] (if (= (n-op f) OP-FUN) (fun-unknown-callers? f) false))
    (None [] true)))

(defn parm-compute [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in p 0)
    (None [] TY-TOP)
    (Some [f]
          (if (!= (n-op f) OP-FUN)
              ;; The Fun has collapsed during inlining but this Parm has not yet taken its turn.
              ;; Preserve the aligned argument type until `parm-idealize` replaces the Parm; TOP
              ;; here would widen an SCCP-sharpened parameter in that intentional transient. If
              ;; the aligned arm was removed in the same fold, there is no value evidence left.
              (if (= (n-nins p) 1)
                  TY-TOP
                  (match (n-in p 1)
                    (Some [v]
                          (if (= (n-ty v) TY-INVALID)
                              (parm-declared p)
                              (ty-join (n-ty v) (parm-declared p))))
                    (None [] TY-TOP)))
              (if (parm-in-progress? p)
                  ;; Unknown callers: the parameter could be anything the annotation allows.
                  (parm-declared p)
                  ;; During trivial inlining the Fun's sole control input has already been
                  ;; rewritten from Call to caller control. It can still be provisionally TOP
                  ;; under SCCP, but the Parm's aligned value is concrete and is about to replace
                  ;; the Parm through the ordinary single-input Phi rule. Final Simple gets this
                  ;; from Parm inheriting Phi.compute while Fun is folding; spell it out here
                  ;; because Coil's Parm compute owns its call-aware live-path filtering.
                  (if (fun-folding? f)
                      (if (= (n-nins p) 1)
                          TY-TOP
                          (match (n-in p 1)
                            (Some [v]
                                  (if (= (n-ty v) TY-INVALID)
                                      (parm-declared p)
                                      (ty-join (n-ty v) (parm-declared p))))
                            (None [] TY-TOP)))
                      ;; Otherwise the meet over LIVE callers, joined with the declared type.
                      (let [cnt (n-nins p)
                            (mut i) 1
                            (mut t) TY-TOP]
                        (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                                  (break 0)
                                  (do
                                    (match (n-in f (load i))
                                      (Some [caller]
                                            (do
                                              (n-add-dep! caller p)
                                              (if (or (= (n-ty caller) TY-XCTRL)
                                                      (= (n-ty caller) TY-TOP))
                                                  0
                                                  (match (n-in p (load i))
                                                    (Some [v]
                                                          (if (= (n-ty v)
                                                                 TY-INVALID)
                                                              0
                                                              (set! (mut t)
                                                                    (ty-meet (load t)
                                                                             (n-ty v)))))
                                                    (None [] 0)))))
                                      (None [] 0))
                                    (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                                    (continue))))
                        ;; `constrain`: never wider than the annotation. During inlining a Call can
                        ;; disappear one worklist turn before this Parm idealizes to its aligned
                        ;; argument. With no remaining live Call control, the fresh meet is TOP; keep
                        ;; the already-proven type for that transient instead of widening back to the
                        ;; declaration and violating the pessimistic monotonicity contract.
                        (let [constrained (ty-join (load t) (parm-declared p))
                              old (n-ty p)]
                          (if (and (= (load t) TY-TOP)
                                   (and (!= old TY-INVALID)
                                        (ty-isa? old constrained)))
                              old
                              constrained)))))))))

(defn parm-idealize [(p (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (n-in p 0)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [f]
          (if (!= (n-op f) OP-FUN)
              ;; The Fun collapsed. A dead function can lose every caller before the Parm is removed,
              ;; and in that transient form there is no replacement input at all.
              (if (= (n-nins p) 1) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]) (n-in p 1))
              ;; >>> THE OTHER HALF OF INLINING <<<
              ;; While the Fun is FOLDING, run the full Phi rules — which is how a Parm collapses
              ;; into the caller's actual argument. Argument substitution IS Phi rule 1.
              (if (fun-folding? f)
                  (phi-single-unique-input p)
                  ;; Otherwise skip the Phi optimisations: a Parm's inputs are call sites, and most
                  ;; Phi rewrites would push work into every caller.
                  (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))

(impl NodeOps ParmNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; While the Fun has unknown callers the Parm reports its DECLARED type, which may well be
  ;; constant. Folding it then would replace a parameter with one caller's value — the engine's
  ;; in-progress hook, reserved for exactly this.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
                  (-> bool)
                  (parm-in-progress? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ParmNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (parm-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ParmNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (parm-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ParmNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-PARM)
                false
                (= (.idx self)
                   (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ParmNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ParmNode))] (-> i64) (+ 179 (.idx self))))

(defn parm-make-slot [(fun (dyn NodeOps))
                      (slot i64)
                      (declared i64)
                      (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ParmNode] (alloc/create [ParmNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) slot)
    (set! (.declared p) declared)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-PARM)
    (n-add-def! d (Some fun))
    ;; The Fun already has its unknown-caller hook at input 1, so the Parm needs a matching
    ;; placeholder to stay positionally aligned.
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (n-set-ty! d declared)
    d))

(defn parm-make [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (declared i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (parm-make-slot fun (+ CALL-ARG0 idx) declared name))

(defn parm-set-unknown-input! [(p (dyn NodeOps)) (v (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-set-def! p 1 (Some v)))

;; Find the Parm with this index on a Fun. There is at most one.
(defn fun-parm [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nouts f)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out f (load i))
                  (Some [u]
                        (if (and (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                                 (= (parm-call-slot u) (+ CALL-ARG0 idx)))
                            (set! (mut found) (Some u))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

(defn fun-mem-parm [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nouts f)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out f (load i))
                  (Some [u]
                        (if (and (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                                 (= (parm-call-slot u) CALL-MEM))
                            (set! (mut found) (Some u))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; The return-program-counter parameter occupies Call slot 0. It is not a source-language
;; argument, so keep its lookup separate from `fun-parm`, whose index is deliberately source based.
(defn fun-rpc-parm [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nouts f)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out f (load i))
                  (Some [u]
                        (if (and (= (n-op u) OP-PARM) (= (parm-call-slot u) 0))
                            (set! (mut found) (Some u))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; FUNPTR — a constant naming a Fun
;;
;; Its type is a `TypeFunPtr` carrying the signature and a SINGLETON function-index set, so a call
;; through it is a direct call.
(defstruct FunPtrNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (fptr-ty i64)])

(impl NodeOps FunPtrNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "FunPtr")
  ;; It IS a constant — of function-pointer type. Saying so stops the engine trying to replace it
  ;; with a Con, which would loop.
  (is-const? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr FunPtrNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> i64) (.fptr-ty self))
  (idealize [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr FunPtrNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-FUNPTR)
                false
                (= (.fptr-ty self)
                   (.fptr-ty (primitive/cast (ptr FunPtrNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr FunPtrNode))] (-> i64) (* (.fptr-ty self) 181)))

;; Our FunPtr has a real Fun input, so signature sharpening can update every pointer directly when
;; `fun-set-sig!` changes the owner. This is the same cached-constant update Simple performs in
;; FunPtr idealization, while preserving the last valid identity if the function body later dies.
(defn funptr-refresh-from-fun! [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
        (mut i) 0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out fun (load i))
                  (Some [use]
                        (if (= (n-op use) OP-FUNPTR)
                            (let [p (primitive/cast (ptr FunPtrNode) (hdr use))
                                  prior (.fptr-ty p)]
                              (if (= prior TY-INVALID)
                                  (panic-i "funptr-refresh-from-fun!: FunPtr payload is TY_INVALID; nid"
                                           (n-nid use))
                                  0)
                              (let [fresh
                                      (ty-funptr sig (ty-funptr-fidxs prior))]
                                (if (= fresh (.fptr-ty p))
                                    0
                                    (do
                                      (n-unlock! use)
                                      (set! (.fptr-ty p) fresh)
                                      (n-force-ty! use fresh)
                                      (code-add! use)
                                      (code-add-outputs! use)
                                      0))))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    0))

(defn funptr-make [(fun (dyn NodeOps)) (fidx i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [FunPtrNode] (alloc/create [FunPtrNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)
        t (ty-funptr-singleton (fun-sig fun) (reserve-fidx! fidx))]
    (arena-register-function! fidx fun)
    (set! (.fptr-ty p) t)
    (n-init! d OP-FUNPTR)
    (n-add-def! d (Some fun))
    (n-set-ty! d t)
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; CALL — a block TAIL
;;
;; Layout: 0 = control, 1 = memory, 2.. = arguments, LAST = the function pointer.
(defstruct CallNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (cend-nid i64)])

(defn call-ctrl [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c CALL-CTRL))

(defn call-mem [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c CALL-MEM))

(defn call-fptr [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c (- (n-nins c) 1)))

(defn call-nargs [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (- (n-nins c) 3))

(defn call-arg [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (i i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! c (+ CALL-ARG0 i)))

(defn call-cend [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  ;; Def-use edges are authoritative after GVN: a CallEnd can be subsumed, making the construction
  ;; back-pointer stale even though the replacement is correctly wired to this Call.
  (let [(mut i) 0
        (mut found) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) (n-nouts c))
                  (match (load found) (Some [_] true) (None [] false)))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out c (load i))
                  (Some [use]
                        (if (and (= (n-op use) OP-CALLEND)
                                 (match (n-in use 0)
                                   (Some [owner] (n-same? owner c))
                                   (None [] false)))
                            (set! (mut found) (Some use))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (match (load found)
      (Some [end] (Some end))
      (None [] (arena-node (.cend-nid (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr c))))))))

(defn call-set-cend! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.cend-nid (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr c))) (n-nid e)) 0))

(impl NodeOps CallNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CallNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Call")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CallNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; A Call is reachable exactly when its control is.
  (compute [(self (ptr CallNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (n-ty (n-in! (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) CALL-CTRL)))
  ;; Final Simple's CallNode idealize discovers the concrete call graph during the pessimistic
  ;; fixpoint. Waiting until optimistic SCCP leaves CallEnds with no linked Return while the same
  ;; fixpoint is already considering them for inlining.
  (idealize [(self (ptr CallNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (let [c (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
              (match (call-cend c)
                (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
                (Some [_]
                      (let [fptr (call-fptr c)
                            ft (n-ty fptr)]
                        (if (and (and (!= ft TY-INVALID)
                                      (and (ty-funptr? ft)
                                           (ty-funptr-single? ft)))
                                 (and (not (ty-high? (n-ty c)))
                                      (= (call-nargs c)
                                         (- (ty-tuple-len (ty-funptr-sig ft)) 1))))
                            ;; Final Simple resolves the target through CodeGen's linker table by
                            ;; function index. The pointer node's input 0 is the Fun only for a
                            ;; FunPtr literal; a singleton-typed Load or Phi has a control input
                            ;; there, and following it would link the ENCLOSING function.
                            (match (arena-function (ty-funptr-the-fidx ft))
                              (Some [fun]
                                    (if (or (!= (n-op fun) OP-FUN)
                                            (not (fun-linkable? fun))
                                            (call-linked? c fun))
                                        (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                                        (do (call-link! c fun) (Some c))))
                              (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
                            (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))))))
  ;; A Call is an effecting CFG site, so two sites must never GVN-merge even when their control,
  ;; memory, arguments and target happen to match. Merging them would execute one call instead of
  ;; two and leave their `cend-nid` back-pointers naming different continuations. Stable call-site
  ;; identity is therefore semantic, not a temporary substitute for memory SSA.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CallNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (and (= (n-op other) OP-CALL)
                 (= (.nid (hdr (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
                    (n-nid other))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CallNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (+ 191 (.nid (hdr (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))))

(impl CFGOps CallNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CallNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A Call is a block TAIL; the CallEnd that follows is the head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CallNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CallNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) CALL-CTRL))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CallNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

;; Build a call site. Arguments are appended, then the function pointer LAST.
(defn call-make-with-mem [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps)) (mem (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CallNode] (alloc/create [CallNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.cend-nid p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-CALL)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d (Some mem))
    (n-set-ty! d (n-ty ctrl))
    d))

;; Legacy graph-construction entry point retained for tests that deliberately exercise incomplete
;; calls. Production lowering uses `call-make-with-mem`; backend handoff rejects a null memory slot.
(defn call-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CallNode] (alloc/create [CallNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.cend-nid p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-CALL)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (n-set-ty! d (n-ty ctrl))
    d))

(defn call-add-arg! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (arg (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-add-def! c (Some arg)))

;; Append the function pointer. Must be LAST — `call-fptr` reads the final input, and `call-nargs`
;; counts everything between the memory slot and it.
(defn call-set-fptr! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fptr (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-add-def! c (Some fptr)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; CALLEND — a block HEAD, and where the inline decision lives
;;
;; Input 0 is the Call. Inputs 1.. are the linked Returns — one per function this call site may
;; reach. Exactly one means a resolved direct call.
(defstruct CallEndNode
  [(hdr NodeHdr) (folding bool) (never-inline bool) (decision i64)])

(defn cend-call [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! e 0))

(defn cend-folding? [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))))

(defn cend-set-folding! [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (v bool)] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.folding (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))) v) 0))

;; Calls that cross a compiler-owned ABI boundary must remain calls even when the body is tiny. The
;; source entry wrapper uses this to keep its boxed JavaScript callee distinct from the process ABI;
;; ordinary user and JSL calls retain final Simple's rationed inlining policy.
(defn callend-set-never-inline! [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.never-inline (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))) true) 0))

(defn cend-decision [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.decision (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))))

;; The result type: a tuple of (control, memory, value), matching Return projection positions.
(defn cend-compute [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in e 0)
    (None [] (ty-tuple-of3 TY-XCTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP))
    (Some [c]
          (if (or (!= (n-op c) OP-CALL) (!= (n-ty c) TY-CTRL))
              (ty-tuple-of3 TY-XCTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)
              (let [fptr (call-fptr c)
                    ft (do (n-add-dep! fptr e) (n-ty fptr))]
                (if (= ft TY-INVALID)
                    (n-ty e)
                    (if (not (ty-funptr? ft))
                        (if (ty-high? ft)
                            (ty-tuple-of3 TY-XCTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)
                            (ty-tuple-of3 TY-CTRL (ty-mem 1 TY-BOT) TY-BOT))
                        ;; While folding, preserve the single linked Return's entire state tuple.
                        (if (cend-folding? e)
                            (match (n-in-safe e 1)
                              (Some [ret]
                                    ;; Return can be collected one worklist turn before CallEnd's
                                    ;; projections finish forwarding. TY-INVALID is a transient graph
                                    ;; state, not lattice evidence; retain the last monotone CallEnd
                                    ;; answer until ordinary folding removes the edge.
                                    (if (= (n-ty ret) TY-INVALID)
                                        (n-ty e)
                                        (cend--state-with-declared-ret (n-ty ret)
                                                                       (ty-sig-ret (ty-funptr-sig ft)))))
                              ;; Final Simple's body copier can omit an external Return from a
                              ;; cloned folding CallEnd. Keep the last monotone answer until that
                              ;; transient data-shaped CallEnd is collected.
                              (None [] (n-ty e)))
                            ;; Otherwise meet the full state tuples from every linked Return.
                            (cend--linked-state e
                                                (ty-sig-ret (ty-funptr-sig ft)))))))))))

;; Preserve control and memory, and constrain only the value member by the declared return type.
(defn cend--state-with-declared-ret [(state i64) (declared i64)] (-> i64)
  (if (and (ty-tuple? state) (> (ty-tuple-len state) RETURN-VALUE))
      (ty-tuple-of3 (ty-tuple-at state RETURN-CTRL)
                    (ty-tuple-at state RETURN-MEM)
                    (ty-join (ty-tuple-at state RETURN-VALUE) declared))
      (ty-tuple-of3 TY-XCTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)))

;; Meet every linked Return's full `(control, memory, value)` state. Before Opto, a missing target
;; may still be linked later, so retain the declared result. During and after Opto, `pesi-ty` is the
;; phase-independent marker that reset-to-top has run; an unresolved call then contributes TOP,
;; exactly as final Simple's `phase >= Opto` branch requires. Returning the declared result during
;; Opto is non-monotone when the newly linked Return is still TOP.
(defn cend--linked-state [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (declared i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [cnt (n-nins e)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut state) (ty-tuple-of3 TY-XCTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)
        (mut any) false]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in e (load i))
                  (Some [r]
                        (let [rt (n-ty r)]
                          (if (and (!= rt TY-INVALID)
                                   (and (ty-tuple? rt)
                                        (> (ty-tuple-len rt) RETURN-VALUE)))
                              (do
                                (set! (mut any) true)
                                (set! (mut state) (ty-meet (load state) rt)))
                              0)))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (if (load any)
        ;; Final Simple preserves a reachable continuation when every linked Return is only
        ;; provisionally unreachable during Opto. Otherwise CallEnd moves CTRL -> XCTRL, tears
        ;; down the caller, and may later need to widen when argument evidence reaches the callee.
        ;; The pessimistic answer is a sound bound: a continuation the parse already proved
        ;; unreachable (a Script that never returns) stays unreachable, or the optimistic answer
        ;; would fall below it.
        (if (and (!= (n-pesi-ty e) TY-INVALID)
                 (= (ty-tuple-at (load state) RETURN-CTRL) TY-XCTRL)
                 (not (and (ty-tuple? (n-pesi-ty e))
                           (= (ty-tuple-at (n-pesi-ty e) RETURN-CTRL) TY-XCTRL))))
            (ty-tuple-of3 TY-CTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)
            (cend--state-with-declared-ret (load state) declared))
        ;; Linked, but every Return is a TY-INVALID transient (a Return being replaced one
        ;; worklist turn before this CallEnd recomputes). That is graph state, not lattice
        ;; evidence: keep the last monotone answer, as the folding branch does.
        (if (and (> cnt 1) (!= (n-ty e) TY-INVALID))
            (n-ty e)
            (if (!= (n-pesi-ty e) TY-INVALID)
                (ty-tuple-of3 TY-CTRL (ty-mem-top 1) TY-TOP)
                (ty-tuple-of3 TY-CTRL (ty-mem 1 TY-BOT) declared))))))

(impl NodeOps CallEndNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
       (-> (ptr NodeHdr))
       (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "CallEnd")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CallEndNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (cend-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  ;; Simple's CallEnd has no `idealize` at all — the inline decision is driven by the SEPARATE inline
  ;; worklist, not by the ordinary peephole. Keeping that split is what rations inlining to one per
  ;; round, with a full cleanup between.
  (idealize [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CallEndNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (= (n-op other) OP-CALLEND))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> i64) 193))

(impl CFGOps CallEndNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
            (-> (dyn NodeOps))
            (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A CallEnd IS a block head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CallEndNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
              ;; While inlining, the Call edge is being folded away. The linked Return is the
              ;; actual control predecessor and must anchor dominance until the fold completes.
              (if (.folding self) (n-in-safe d 1) (n-in-safe d 0))))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CallEndNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn callend-make [(c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CallEndNode] (alloc/create [CallEndNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.folding p) false)
    (set! (.never-inline p) false)
    (set! (.decision p) INLINE-DEFER)
    (n-init! d OP-CALLEND)
    (n-add-def! d (Some c))
    (call-set-cend! c d)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; LINKING — the call graph is DISCOVERED, not declared
;;
;; Simple's explanation, worth keeping: the Call->Fun edges "are not in from the start because they
;; are *too many*; we would get O(n^2) edges. So all along we have been treating them as virtual CFG
;; edges — a FunNode taking a Start input is treated as if every call on the planet might call it.
;; Now we make these CG edges concrete, as we discover that a call calls a particular function."
;;
;; A link is TWO edges:
;;   Call  -> Fun      (the Fun gains a caller; every Parm gains that caller's argument)
;;   Return-> CallEnd  (the CallEnd gains a return to meet)
;;
;; Because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is a Phi, argument types then flow into the callee through
;; the ORDINARY merge rules, with no interprocedural machinery.
(defn call-link! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do
    ;; The Fun gains a caller.
    (n-add-def! fun (Some c))
    ;; EVERY Parm gains the matching argument, in the SAME operation — the Region/Phi arity
    ;; invariant. A Parm left one value short reads the wrong caller's argument.
    (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
          (mut i) 0]
      (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                (break 0)
                (do
                  (match (n-out fun (load i))
                    (Some [u]
                          (if (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                              (let [slot (parm-call-slot u)]
                                (if (and (>= slot CALL-MEM)
                                         (< slot (- (n-nins c) 1)))
                                    (n-add-def! u (n-in c slot))
                                    ;; The caller passes fewer arguments than the callee declares.
                                    ;; That is an arity error, not something to paper over with a
                                    ;; default: link nothing and let the type check report it.
                                    (n-add-def! u (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
                                ;; `n-add-def!` maintains edges but intentionally does not schedule.
                                ;; Linking changes this Phi's meet, so SCCP must revisit it now;
                                ;; its users are queued when the resulting type sharpens.
                                (code-add! u))
                              0))
                    (None [] 0))
                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                  (continue)))))
    ;; The CallEnd gains the callee's Return and must recompute from that new full state tuple.
    (match (call-cend c)
      (Some [e]
            (do
              (match (fun-ret fun)
                (Some [r] (n-add-def! e (Some r)))
                (None [] 0))
              (code-add! e)))
      (None [] 0))
    (code-add! fun)
    (code-add! c)
    0))

;; Remove one concrete call-graph edge, preserving the Fun/Parm positional invariant and the
;; Return/CallEnd half of the link. Clone inlining uses this before retargeting one caller.
(defn call-unlink! [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [cnt (n-nins fun)
        (mut path) -1
        (mut i) 1]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in fun (load i))
                  (Some [caller]
                        (if (n-same? caller c) (set! (mut path) (load i)) 0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (if (< (load path) 0)
        (broken "call-unlink!: call is not linked to function")
        (region-del-path! fun (load path)))
    (match (call-cend c)
      (Some [e]
            (match (fun-ret fun)
              (Some [ret]
                    (let [(mut j) 1
                          (mut removed) false]
                      (loop (if (or (>= (load j) (n-nins e)) (load removed))
                                (break 0)
                                (do
                                  (match (n-in e (load j))
                                    (Some [linked]
                                          (if (n-same? linked ret)
                                              (do
                                                (n-del-def! e (load j))
                                                (set! (mut removed) true)
                                                0)
                                              0))
                                    (None [] 0))
                                  (if (load removed)
                                      0
                                      (set! (mut j) (+ (load j) 1)))
                                  (continue))))
                      (if (load removed)
                          0
                          ;; A Return can be folded from CallEnd one sparse-worklist turn before
                          ;; the now-unreachable Call is removed from Fun. The caller half still
                          ;; has to be deleted; absence here means that half is already unlinked.
                          0)))
              (None [] (broken "call-unlink!: function has no Return"))))
      ;; A folding CallEnd can finish disappearing before its obsolete caller edge takes a final
      ;; worklist turn. The Return half is already gone in that shape; the Fun/Parm half above still
      ;; has to be removed. Final Simple cannot observe a half-link because its Call always retains
      ;; its CallEnd, but its unlink driver likewise treats Fun caller inputs as authoritative.
      (None [] 0))
    (code-add! fun)
    (code-add! c)
    0))

;; Final Simple runs `unlink` after serialization and before instruction selection. The optimizer's
;; Call -> Fun and Return -> CallEnd discovery edges are not runtime control flow; leaving them in
;; the selected graph creates cross-function liveness and lets GCM enter a callee from its caller.
;; Iterate the live Fun caller inputs, exactly as final Simple's `CodeGen.unlink`, instead of looking
;; up a target through the Call's function pointer. A folding CallEnd can disappear before its Call,
;; and a copied Call can retain only the Call->Fun half temporarily; skipping that CallEnd-invalid
;; shape leaks optimizer control into selection. The Fun inputs are the authoritative concrete call
;; graph in both cases. The FunPtr input remains and supplies the machine symbol/fidx.
(defn call-unlink-all! [] (-> i64)
  (let [limit (arena-node-count)
        (mut nid) 1
        (mut removed) 0]
    (loop (if (> (load nid) limit)
              (break (load removed))
              (do
                (match (arena-node (load nid))
                  (Some [fun]
                        (if (and (= (n-op fun) OP-FUN) (not (n-dead? fun)))
                            (let [(mut i) 1]
                              (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins fun))
                                        (break 0)
                                        (match (n-in fun (load i))
                                          (Some [caller]
                                                (if (= (n-op caller) OP-CALL)
                                                    (do
                                                      (call-unlink! caller fun)
                                                      (set! (mut removed)
                                                            (+ (load removed) 1))
                                                      ;; Region/Parm arms compressed; examine this
                                                      ;; same index again.
                                                      (continue))
                                                    (do
                                                      (set! (mut i)
                                                            (+ (load i) 1))
                                                      (continue))))
                                          (None []
                                                (do
                                                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                                                  (continue)))))))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut nid) (+ (load nid) 1))
                (continue))))))

;; Has this concrete call-graph edge already been installed?  Fun inputs are the authoritative
;; call graph; checking them prevents SCCP revisits from duplicating Region/Parm paths.
(defn call-linked? [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [cnt (n-nins fun)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut found) false]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (load found))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in fun (load i))
                  (Some [x] (if (n-same? x c) (set! (mut found) true) 0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE INLINE DECISION  —  Simple's `CallEndNode.maybeInline` / `inlineCandidate`
;;
;; A TRI-STATE that DEFERS rather than declines:
;;
;;   INLINE-TRIVIAL ( 1)  inline now, by folding
;;   INLINE-CLONE   ( 2)  clone the body first, then fold
;;   INLINE-DEFER   (-1)  NOT NOW, but might become one — re-queued when the blocker changes
;;   INLINE-NEVER   (-2)  never; stop asking
;;
;; `-1` is our "deferred, never declined" law appearing in Simple's own code. Each deferral registers
;; a dependency on whatever blocked it, so nothing has to remember to come back.

;; Decide WITHOUT acting, setting no flags. Split out because the expensive-assert path must be able
;; to ask the question with no side effects.
(defn callend-inline-candidate [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (.never-inline (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e)))
      INLINE-NEVER
      (if (cend-folding? e)
          INLINE-NEVER  ; already folding, and will collapse anyway
          (match (n-in-safe e 0)
            (None [] INLINE-NEVER)  ; malformed, mid-death
            (Some [c]
                  (if (!= (n-op c) OP-CALL)
                      INLINE-NEVER
                      ;; Not exactly ONE linked target yet. SCCP may still link one, so DEFER.
                      (if (!= (n-nins e) 2)
                          INLINE-DEFER
                          (callend--candidate-with-target e c (n-in! e 1)))))))))

;; Evidence that inlining a JSL body can fold something: an argument whose type is strictly
;; sharper than the formal it meets (a discharged annotation, a literal, a known shape, a constant),
;; or a body too small to be worth a call. Without evidence every argument and the Fun are
;; registered as dependencies, so the question is reasked when SCCP or an outer inline sharpens one.
(defn callend--inline-evidence? [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps)) (e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [sig (fun-sig fun)
        n (ty-sig-nargs sig)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut evidence) false]
    (if (and (= (call-nargs c) n) (<= (fun-live-body-size fun) INLINE-TINY-SIZE))
        (set! (mut evidence) true)
        0)
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) n) (load evidence) (!= (call-nargs c) n))
              (break 0)
              (let [arg (call-arg c (load i))
                    at (n-ty arg)
                    formal (ty-sig-arg sig (load i))]
                (if (and (!= at TY-INVALID) (ty-isa? at formal) (!= at formal))
                    (set! (mut evidence) true)
                    (n-add-dep! arg e))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (if (load evidence) 0 (n-add-dep! fun e))
    (load evidence)))

(defn callend--candidate-with-target [(e (dyn NodeOps))
                                      (c (dyn NodeOps))
                                      (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  ;; A linked Return can finish folding one worklist turn before the CallEnd and its projections.
  ;; Its arena identity remains available as ownership metadata, but it is not an inline target.
  (if (= (n-ty ret) TY-INVALID)
      INLINE-DEFER
      (match (return-fun ret)
        (None [] INLINE-DEFER)
        (Some [fun]
              ;; A value-taken function inlines like any other: every call through its object is a
              ;; linked site in the closed world, and a Fun left without callers is a dead target
              ;; whose code word names the runtime trap.
              (if (fun-no-inline? fun)
                  INLINE-NEVER
                  ;; A JSL definition is a generic abstract operation: its body is a type dispatch,
                  ;; and inlining it where no argument is sharper than its formal buys the dispatch
                  ;; tree at the site instead of a call to the same tree (docs/DECISIONS.md, the
                  ;; compile-time architecture, decision 3). Defer on the arguments and the Fun so a
                  ;; sharpening reasks; a site that never earns its inline stays a call to the one
                  ;; shared body. Asked first: it is cheap, and the deferred set is reasked often.
                  (if (and (fun-jsl? fun) (not (callend--inline-evidence? c fun e)))
                      INLINE-DEFER
                  (if (not (call-linked? c fun))
                      (do (n-add-dep! fun e) INLINE-DEFER)
                      ;; SELF-RECURSION is never inlined — that is loop unrolling by another name. But the
                      ;; recursive edge might yet disappear, so DEFER rather than refuse forever, and depend on
                      ;; the Fun so the question is reasked if its callers change.
                      (if (fun-self-recursive? fun)
                          (do (n-add-dep! fun e) INLINE-DEFER)
                          (let [fptr (call-fptr c)
                                ft (n-ty fptr)]
                            ;; The function pointer must be a CONSTANT, non-empty, singleton set. Anything else
                            ;; may still sharpen, so depend on it and defer.
                            (if (or (= ft TY-INVALID)
                                    (not (and (ty-funptr? ft)
                                              (ty-funptr-single? ft))))
                                (do (n-add-dep! fptr e) INLINE-DEFER)
                                ;; Arguments must match the formals. `call-good-args?` depends on any that do
                                ;; not yet.
                                (if (not (call-good-args? c (fun-sig fun) e))
                                    INLINE-DEFER
                                    ;; SIZE. Over the hard cap: never. Under it: trivial when this is the only
                                    ;; caller, otherwise the clone path.
                                    (if (>= (fun-approx-size fun)
                                            INLINE-MAX-SIZE)
                                        INLINE-NEVER
                                        (if (>= (fun-live-body-size fun)
                                                INLINE-MAX-SIZE)
                                            (do (n-add-dep! fun e) INLINE-DEFER)
                                            (if (fun-unknown-callers? fun)
                                                (do
                                                  (n-add-dep! fun e)
                                                  INLINE-DEFER)
                                                (if (> (n-nins fun) 2)
                                                    INLINE-CLONE
                                                    INLINE-TRIVIAL)))))))))))))))
;; The Fun a Return belongs to is metadata, not an input edge. Simple likewise keeps `_fun`
;; separately from input 3, which is the RPC edge cleared during inlining.
(defn return-fun [(ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [nid (return-fun-nid ret)]
    (if (= nid 0)
        (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
        (match (arena-node nid)
          (Some [f] (if (= (n-op f) OP-FUN) (Some f) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
          (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))

;; Does this Fun call itself? Simple walks the Fun's caller inputs looking for one inside the Fun.
;; Our call graph is linked by the same worklist that asks the inline question, so a direct self
;; FunPtr can be visible one turn before its Call has become a Fun input. Inspecting the Fun's sparse
;; FunPtr outputs closes that ordering window without scanning the arena or inventing a second call
;; graph. Once linked, the first loop remains the authoritative Simple path.
;; Arena-indexed marks for the control-ownership walk, process-wide scratch grown to the arena once
;; and cleared through the ids a walk set; a fresh arena-sized map per walk, per candidate check,
;; was the largest single cost of a compile.
(defstruct ControlOwnedScratch
  [(seen (ArrayList bool)) (touched (ArrayList i64)) (booted bool)])

(defn control-owned-scratch [] (-> (ptr ControlOwnedScratch))
  (primitive/alloc-static ControlOwnedScratch))

(defn fun--control-owned? [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (start (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        s (control-owned-scratch)
        limit (+ (arena-node-count) 1)
        (mut work) (al-new [(dyn NodeOps)] a)
        (mut yes) false]
    (if (.booted s)
        0
        (do
          (set! (.seen s) (al-new [bool] a))
          (set! (.touched s) (al-new [i64] a))
          (set! (.booted s) true)
          0))
    (do
      (loop (if (>= (len (.seen s)) limit)
                (break 0)
                (do (push! [bool] (mut (.seen s)) false) (continue))))
      (push! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut work) start)
      (loop (if (or (= (len (load work)) 0) (load yes))
                (break 0)
                (match (pop! (mut work))
                  (None [] (break 0))
                  (Some [n]
                        (let [id (n-nid n)]
                          (if (or (<= id 0) (>= id limit) (get (.seen s) id))
                              0
                              (do
                                (set! (mut (.seen s)) id true)
                                (push! [i64] (mut (.touched s)) id)
                                (if (n-same? n f)
                                    (set! (mut yes) true)
                                    (if (= (n-op n) OP-FUN)
                                        0
                                        ;; This is control ownership, not arbitrary graph reachability.
                                        ;; Simple's recursion guard walks the caller's dominators. A
                                        ;; CallEnd also references its callee Return; following that
                                        ;; edge misclassifies two sequential calls as self-recursion.
                                        ;; Before dominators settle, visit all Region predecessors but
                                        ;; only input zero of other control nodes (CallEnd -> Call).
                                        (let [merge
                                                (or (= (n-op n) OP-REGION)
                                                    (= (n-op n) OP-LOOP))
                                              limit
                                                (if merge
                                                    (n-nins n)
                                                    (if (> (n-nins n) 0) 1 0))
                                              (mut input) (if merge 1 0)]
                                          (loop (if (>= (load input) limit)
                                                    (break 0)
                                                    (do
                                                      (match (n-in n
                                                                   (load input))
                                                        (Some [def]
                                                              (push! [(dyn NodeOps)]
                                                                     (mut work)
                                                                     def))
                                                        (None [] 0))
                                                      (set! (mut input)
                                                            (+ (load input) 1))
                                                      (continue)))))))))
                          (continue))))))
      (al-free! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut work))
      (al-for [id (.touched s)] (set! (mut (.seen s)) id false))
      (al-clear! [i64] (mut (.touched s)))
      (load yes))))

;; Memoized per inlining epoch (see `fun-size-epoch`): every deferred candidate asks each round, and
;; the answer only changes when the graph does.
(defn fun-self-recursive? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [p (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr f))
        epoch (+ (load (fun-size-epoch)) 1)]
    (if (= (.self-recursive-epoch p) epoch)
        (.self-recursive p)
        (let [answer (fun--self-recursive-walk? f)]
          (set! (.self-recursive p) answer)
          (set! (.self-recursive-epoch p) epoch)
          answer))))

(defn fun--self-recursive-walk? [(f (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [cnt (n-nins f)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut yes) false]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (load yes))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in f (load i))
                  (Some [caller]
                        (if (= (n-op caller) OP-CALL)
                            (match (call-cend caller)
                              (Some [ce]
                                    (if (fun--reaches? f ce)
                                        (set! (mut yes) true)
                                        0))
                              (None [] 0))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (set! (mut i) 0)
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) (n-nouts f)) (load yes))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out f (load i))
                  (Some [fp]
                        (if (= (n-op fp) OP-FUNPTR)
                            (let [(mut oi) 0]
                              (loop (if (or (>= (load oi) (n-nouts fp))
                                            (load yes))
                                        (break 0)
                                        (do
                                          (match (n-out fp (load oi))
                                            (Some [use_]
                                                  (if (= (n-op use_) OP-CALL)
                                                      (if (fun--control-owned? f
                                                                               (call-ctrl use_))
                                                          (set! (mut yes) true)
                                                          0)
                                                      0))
                                            (None [] 0))
                                          (set! (mut oi) (+ (load oi) 1))
                                          (continue)))))
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load yes)))

;; Is `ce` inside `f`'s body? Walks the dominator chain up looking for the Fun.
(defn fun--reaches? [(f (dyn NodeOps)) (ce (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [(mut cur) (Some ce)
        (mut yes) false
        (mut guard) 0]
    (loop (if (> (load guard) 100000)
              (do
                (broken "fun--reaches?: dominator walk did not converge")
                (break 0))
              (match (load cur)
                (None [] (break 0))
                (Some [n]
                      (if (n-same? n f)
                          (do (set! (mut yes) true) (break 0))
                          (do
                            (set! (mut cur) (cfg-idom-of n))
                            (set! (mut guard) (+ (load guard) 1))
                            (continue)))))))
    (load yes)))

;; Does every argument's type `isa` its formal? Depends on any that does not yet, so the candidate is
;; retried when it sharpens.
(defn call-good-args? [(c (dyn NodeOps)) (sig i64) (dep (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [n (ty-sig-nargs sig)]
    (if (!= (call-nargs c) n)
        false  ; arity mismatch: an error, never inlinable
        (let [(mut i) 0
              (mut ok) true]
          (loop (if (or (>= (load i) n) (not (load ok)))
                    (break 0)
                    (do
                      (let [arg (call-arg c (load i))
                            formal (ty-sig-arg sig (load i))]
                        (if (= (n-ty arg) TY-INVALID)
                            (set! (mut ok) false)
                            (if (ty-isa? (n-ty arg) formal)
                                0
                                (do
                                  (n-add-dep! arg dep)
                                  (set! (mut ok) false)
                                  0))))
                      (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                      (continue))))
          (load ok)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; TRIVIAL INLINING — read this twice: it CLONES NOTHING
;;
;; Simple's comment: "Inlining does not need to clone code, merely triggers folding the Call/Fun and
;; Return/CallEnd away." The entire operation is:
;;
;;     cend.folding = true
;;     fun.folding  = true
;;     fun.setDef(1, call.ctrl())    ; the Fun's control now BYPASSES the Call
;;     fun.ret().setDef(3, null)     ; the Return's RPC edge is consumed by the inline
;;     re-queue fun, cend, their neighbours, and the Parms' users one layer down
;;     invalidate the idepth caches
;;
;; ...and then it STOPS. The ORDINARY peepholes do the rest, because a Fun is a Region and a Parm is
;; a Phi:
;;
;;     the Fun now has ONE input   -> Region rule 3 collapses it into the caller's control
;;     each Parm has ONE value     -> Phi rule 1 collapses it into the caller's argument
;;
;; Argument substitution, control splicing and return merging are all just those two existing rules
;; firing. There is no inliner body to write.
;;
;; The Parms' users must be re-queued one layer down, because a Parm collapsing changes the types of
;; everything reading it and those nodes are two steps from the Fun.
(defn callend-do-trivial-inline! [(e (dyn NodeOps)) (fun (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [c (cend-call e)]
    (cend-set-folding! e true)
    (fun-set-folding! fun true)
    ;; Bypass the Call: the Fun's single caller edge becomes the caller's own control.
    (n-set-def! fun 1 (Some (call-ctrl c)))
    ;; Clear Return's RPC exactly when the call is folded into its caller. Fun ownership metadata
    ;; remains intact.
    (match (fun-ret fun)
      (Some [ret] (n-set-def! ret RETURN-RPC (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
      (None [] 0))
    ;; Re-queue the neighbourhood, and the Parms' users one layer down.
    (code-add! fun)
    (code-add! e)
    (code-add-outputs! fun)
    (let [cnt (n-nouts fun)
          (mut i) 0]
      (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                (break 0)
                (do
                  (match (n-out fun (load i))
                    (Some [u]
                          (if (= (n-op u) OP-PARM)
                              (do
                                ;; Queue the changing Phi itself as well as its users. Relying on
                                ;; the generic Fun-output enqueue is insufficient when that entry
                                ;; was already present before the inline worklist interrupts the
                                ;; ordinary worklist; the sparse set would otherwise consume the
                                ;; old entry and never revisit the now-folding Parm.
                                (code-add! u)
                                (code-add-outputs! u))
                              0))
                    (None [] 0))
                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                  (continue)))))
    (code-add-outputs! e)
    (code-add-inputs! e)
    ;; Inlining blows every cached idepth past the inline point. Bump the version rather than hunting
    ;; for the stale ones.
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    0))

;; Decide and ACT. The clone implementation lives in the graph copier, which imports this module;
;; its boot-installed callback preserves an acyclic module graph while keeping this public contract
;; complete for direct callers and IterPeeps alike.
(defn callend-maybe-inline! [(e (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [d (callend-inline-candidate e)]
    (if (mid-assert?)
        d  ; an expensive assert must not mutate
        (do
          (set! (.decision (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode) (hdr e))) d)
          (if (> d 0)
              (match (n-in e 1)
                (Some [ret]
                      (match (return-fun ret)
                        (Some [fun]
                              (if (= d INLINE-TRIVIAL)
                                  (callend-do-trivial-inline! e fun)
                                  (callend-do-clone-inline-ext! e fun)))
                        (None [] 0)))
                (None [] 0))
              0)
          d))))

;; THE CLONE PATH (`INLINE-CLONE`). When the callee has OTHER callers, a trivial fold would steal the
;; body from them. Simple first unlinks the call, copies the body (`fun.copyBody()`), builds a fresh
;; FunPtr over the clone with an INLINE-ONLY function index — one never written to an object file —
;; relinks the call to the clone, and then runs the SAME trivial inline on it. One mechanism, always;
;; cloning is a pre-step, not a second path.
;;
;; `aot.node.copy` implements that pre-step with the same two-pass copier used by serialization and
;; registers it here at boot. `callend-maybe-inline!` invokes the callback before returning
;; INLINE-CLONE; IterPeeps needs no second, secret action and the module graph remains acyclic.
;;
;; MEMORY THREADING. `CALL-MEM` carries the current memory state, Return contributes the updated
;; state, and CallEnd projects it for the caller. Nothing here renumbers around memory.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The control kinds this module owns, contributed to `cfg-of`
;;
;; `Fun`, `Call` and `CallEnd` are control. `Parm` is NOT — it is a Phi, and Phis are data.
(defn cfg-of-calls [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn CFGOps)))
  (let [op (n-op n)]
    (if (= op OP-FUN)
        (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr FunNode) (hdr n))))
        (if (= op OP-CALL)
            (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps
                                      (primitive/cast (ptr CallNode) (hdr n))))
            (if (= op OP-CALLEND)
                (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps
                                          (primitive/cast (ptr CallEndNode)
                                                          (hdr n))))
                (prop-access-cfg n))))))

;; Simple's CallEndNode.pcopy: once folding begins, the CallEnd tuple is its single linked Return.
;; Projection idealization asks this each time, so projections created after the inline decision
;; forward just as reliably as ones already present.
(defn pcopy-of-calls [(n (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (or (= (n-op n) OP-PROPLOAD) (= (n-op n) OP-PROPSTORE))
      (prop-access-pcopy n idx)
      (if (and (= (n-op n) OP-CALLEND) (cend-folding? n))
          (match (n-in-safe n 1)
            (Some [ret] (n-in ret idx))
            (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))
          (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))

;; Install the extension. `aot.node.boot` calls this; reaching a call node's control question before
;; it runs is a named panic, not a silent "not control".
(defn ctrl-install-call-ext! [] (-> i64)
  (do
    (set-cfg-ext! (primitive/fnptr-of cfg-of-calls))
    (set-pcopy-ext! (primitive/fnptr-of pcopy-of-calls))))
